1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,684
Two equations, two unknowns.

2
00:00:02,684 --> 00:00:10,238
Each equation is a straight line, and the solution is where the lines cross: here, the single point two, three.

3
00:00:10,467 --> 00:00:14,661
But turn the second line until it runs parallel to the first.

4
00:00:14,661 --> 00:00:17,212
Now they never meet: no solution at all.

5
00:00:17,433 --> 00:00:23,190
Slide it onto the first, and every point of the line is a solution: infinitely many.

6
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:28,659
With three unknowns the lines become planes, and guessing by eye is hopeless.

7
00:00:28,659 --> 00:00:32,712
This unit gives you machines that decide, and solve, every time.

8
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:44,930
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 5: Matrix Based System of Linear Equations.

9
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:48,382
Four parts.

10
00:00:48,382 --> 00:00:51,286
Cramer's rule, using determinants.

11
00:00:51,286 --> 00:00:53,864
The row equivalent, or Gauss, method.

12
00:00:53,864 --> 00:00:55,687
The inverse matrix method.

13
00:00:55,687 --> 00:01:00,713
And consistency: how to tell one solution from infinitely many from none.

14
00:01:05,067 --> 00:01:08,168
First, every method starts the same way.

15
00:01:08,168 --> 00:01:18,436
Line up the unknowns in the same order and read off three objects: the coefficient matrix A, the column of unknowns X, and the column of constants B.

16
00:01:18,667 --> 00:01:21,715
Why does A X equal B say the same thing?

17
00:01:21,715 --> 00:01:28,460
Multiply the first row of A into the column X: x plus two y plus z.

18
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:30,947
That is the left side of the first equation.

19
00:01:30,947 --> 00:01:33,431
A missing unknown simply gets a zero.

20
00:01:34,633 --> 00:01:37,916
Cramer's rule comes from plain elimination.

21
00:01:37,916 --> 00:01:44,296
Multiply to kill y, subtract, and x times a determinant equals another determinant.

22
00:01:44,296 --> 00:01:47,734
The bottom one is D, made of the coefficients.

23
00:01:47,734 --> 00:01:52,017
The top one is D with the x column replaced by the constants.

24
00:01:52,017 --> 00:01:59,212
So x is D x over D, and y is D y over D, as long as D is not zero.

25
00:02:00,433 --> 00:02:01,703
The cold open's system.

26
00:02:01,933 --> 00:02:05,711
D is minus two minus nine: minus eleven.

27
00:02:05,711 --> 00:02:08,821
Not zero, so there is exactly one solution.

28
00:02:09,033 --> 00:02:13,579
Put thirteen and three in the x column: minus twenty two.

29
00:02:13,579 --> 00:02:16,117
In the y column: minus thirty three.

30
00:02:16,333 --> 00:02:19,056
Divide: x is two and y is three.

31
00:02:19,056 --> 00:02:20,393
The crossing point we saw.

32
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:27,922
With three unknowns the rule is the same, with three by three determinants.

33
00:02:27,922 --> 00:02:31,295
Expand D along its first row: six.

34
00:02:31,295 --> 00:02:32,637
Not zero, so go on.

35
00:02:32,867 --> 00:02:38,611
For D x, copy D and slide the constants into the first column, the x column.

36
00:02:38,611 --> 00:02:39,925
Expand: twelve.

37
00:02:40,133 --> 00:02:44,635
For D y, the constants go into the second column: minus six.

38
00:02:44,867 --> 00:02:48,501
And for D z, the third column: eighteen.

39
00:02:48,501 --> 00:02:50,558
Replace a column, never a row.

40
00:02:50,767 --> 00:02:56,289
Divide each by D: x is two, y is minus one, z is three.

41
00:02:56,289 --> 00:03:01,016
Check in the third equation: four minus one minus three is zero.

42
00:03:01,016 --> 00:03:01,456
Correct.

43
00:03:02,667 --> 00:03:03,400
A word problem.

44
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:07,568
Let apples cost x rupees a kilo and oranges y.

45
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:13,757
D is minus five, D x minus five fifty, D y minus seven hundred.

46
00:03:13,967 --> 00:03:19,138
So apples cost a hundred and ten rupees a kilo, and oranges a hundred and forty.

47
00:03:19,138 --> 00:03:22,255
Check: two twenty plus four twenty is six forty.

48
00:03:24,267 --> 00:03:25,297
One warning.

49
00:03:25,297 --> 00:03:29,516
If D is zero, Cramer's rule says nothing at all.

50
00:03:29,516 --> 00:03:35,045
The system has either no solution or infinitely many, and part four shows how to tell.

51
00:03:39,833 --> 00:03:50,850
The second method uses three elementary row operations: swap two rows, multiply a row by a non zero number, and add a multiple of one row to another.

52
00:03:50,850 --> 00:03:56,475
They are exactly what you do when you eliminate by hand, so they never change the answer.

53
00:03:56,475 --> 00:04:00,827
We apply them to the augmented matrix: A with the constants attached.

54
00:04:02,033 --> 00:04:12,048
Solve x plus two y plus z equals eight, two x plus three y minus z equals five, three x minus y plus two z equals seven.

55
00:04:12,048 --> 00:04:13,625
Write the augmented matrix.

56
00:04:13,833 --> 00:04:16,777
Clear the first column below the leading one.

57
00:04:16,777 --> 00:04:19,694
Row two minus two times row one.

58
00:04:19,694 --> 00:04:22,333
Row three minus three times row one.

59
00:04:22,567 --> 00:04:26,267
Now clear the second column below its pivot, minus one.

60
00:04:26,267 --> 00:04:29,261
Row three minus seven times row two.

61
00:04:29,261 --> 00:04:33,110
The coefficient part is now triangular: echelon form.

62
00:04:33,333 --> 00:04:35,704
Back substitution, from the bottom up.

63
00:04:35,704 --> 00:04:39,754
Twenty z is sixty, so z is three.

64
00:04:39,754 --> 00:04:44,415
Then minus y minus nine is minus eleven, so y is two.

65
00:04:44,415 --> 00:04:48,529
Then x plus four plus three is eight, so x is one.

66
00:04:49,733 --> 00:04:51,584
Gauss Jordan goes further.

67
00:04:51,584 --> 00:04:55,015
Make every pivot one, then clear above the pivots too.

68
00:04:55,233 --> 00:04:58,529
Clear above the third pivot, then above the second.

69
00:04:58,529 --> 00:05:05,117
The left block becomes the identity, and the answer, one, two, three, sits in the last column.

70
00:05:05,117 --> 00:05:06,707
No back substitution at all.

71
00:05:07,933 --> 00:05:09,863
A zero where the first pivot should be.

72
00:05:10,067 --> 00:05:12,452
No problem: swap the first two rows.

73
00:05:12,667 --> 00:05:14,785
Clear the first column, then the second.

74
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,212
Six z is eighteen, so z is three; then y is two and x is one.

75
00:05:23,833 --> 00:05:25,189
Exam tip.

76
00:05:25,189 --> 00:05:28,392
A zero in the pivot place is not a mistake.

77
00:05:28,392 --> 00:05:30,996
Swap with a lower row and carry on.

78
00:05:30,996 --> 00:05:34,993
Only if the whole column below is zero is something special happening.

79
00:05:40,333 --> 00:05:44,073
The third method undoes A. Start from A X equals B.

80
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:52,113
Multiply both sides on the left by A inverse, which exists exactly when the determinant of A is not zero.

81
00:05:52,333 --> 00:05:58,433
A inverse times A is the identity, and the identity times X is X.

82
00:05:58,633 --> 00:06:01,330
So X is A inverse B.

83
00:06:01,330 --> 00:06:09,539
And since A inverse is the adjoint over the determinant, X is one over the determinant of A, times adjoint A, times B.

84
00:06:10,767 --> 00:06:13,855
For a two by two matrix there is a quick recipe.

85
00:06:13,855 --> 00:06:19,883
Swap the two diagonal entries, a and d. Change the signs of the other two.

86
00:06:19,883 --> 00:06:21,120
That is the adjoint.

87
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:25,363
Then divide by the determinant, a d minus b c.

88
00:06:26,567 --> 00:06:28,488
The same system, a third way.

89
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:31,071
Determinant minus eleven.

90
00:06:31,071 --> 00:06:33,916
Swap and negate, then divide by minus eleven.

91
00:06:34,133 --> 00:06:39,200
Multiply into the constants: twenty two and thirty three, over eleven.

92
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:41,043
Two and three, as before.

93
00:06:43,067 --> 00:06:44,078
A three by three.

94
00:06:44,300 --> 00:06:47,674
Note the zero for the missing y in the second equation.

95
00:06:47,674 --> 00:06:48,677
Determinant four.

96
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:53,600
Find all nine cofactors and transpose them: that is the adjoint.

97
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:56,601
Multiply by B and divide by four.

98
00:06:56,601 --> 00:06:59,062
The numbers are three, one and two.

99
00:06:59,062 --> 00:07:01,788
Check: three plus four is seven.

100
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:04,978
A real-world system.

101
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:08,496
Determinant four, so one solution exists.

102
00:07:08,496 --> 00:07:11,637
The inverse method works, but look first for a shortcut.

103
00:07:11,867 --> 00:07:18,783
Adding all three equations gives four times x plus y plus z: thirty one thousand.

104
00:07:18,783 --> 00:07:23,649
So x plus y plus z is seven thousand seven hundred and fifty.

105
00:07:23,867 --> 00:07:31,270
Subtract it from each equation: single twelve fifty, double twenty two fifty, deluxe forty two fifty.

106
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:34,539
Two warnings.

107
00:07:34,539 --> 00:07:38,940
Multiply on the left: B times A inverse is not even defined.

108
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:43,584
And find the determinant first: if it is zero, there is no inverse.

109
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:45,726
Which method?

110
00:07:45,726 --> 00:07:50,414
Cramer needs a non zero determinant and is quickest for small systems.

111
00:07:50,414 --> 00:08:03,109
The inverse also needs a non zero determinant, and pays off when many systems share the same A. Row reduction needs nothing, and it is the only one that still works when the determinant is zero.

112
00:08:08,900 --> 00:08:12,847
Row reduce the augmented matrix, and look at the last row.

113
00:08:12,847 --> 00:08:14,969
It can only look one of three ways.

114
00:08:14,969 --> 00:08:18,117
A non zero pivot: one unique solution.

115
00:08:18,333 --> 00:08:24,025
A whole row of zeros, zero equals zero: true but empty.

116
00:08:24,025 --> 00:08:30,373
Only two real equations remain for three unknowns, so there are infinitely many solutions.

117
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:35,120
Zeros on the left but not on the right: zero equals one.

118
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:36,292
Impossible.

119
00:08:36,292 --> 00:08:39,954
The equations contradict each other, and there is no solution.

120
00:08:40,167 --> 00:08:42,460
The determinant gives the first split.

121
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:44,843
Not zero: one solution.

122
00:08:44,843 --> 00:08:48,502
Zero: then row reduce to tell many from none.

123
00:08:48,502 --> 00:08:57,715
A homogeneous system, with B all zeros, is never inconsistent, because zero, zero, zero always works.

124
00:08:58,933 --> 00:08:59,948
Many solutions.

125
00:09:00,167 --> 00:09:03,662
The determinant is zero, so no unique solution.

126
00:09:03,867 --> 00:09:10,353
Row reduce: the second and third rows become identical, and the last row vanishes.

127
00:09:10,353 --> 00:09:11,852
Zero equals zero.

128
00:09:12,067 --> 00:09:15,076
Two equations, three unknowns.

129
00:09:15,076 --> 00:09:17,329
Let z be any number t.

130
00:09:17,329 --> 00:09:21,811
Then y is eight minus two t, and x is t minus two.

131
00:09:22,033 --> 00:09:29,561
A whole line of solutions, one for every real t. Put t equal to two: zero, four, two.

132
00:09:29,561 --> 00:09:31,774
Check: six, twenty, fourteen.

133
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:34,652
Change one number.

134
00:09:35,033 --> 00:09:38,919
Same coefficients, so the determinant is still zero.

135
00:09:39,133 --> 00:09:43,864
This time the two reduced rows differ in the constant: eight and nine.

136
00:09:44,067 --> 00:09:46,842
Subtract: zero equals one.

137
00:09:46,842 --> 00:09:48,014
Impossible.

138
00:09:48,014 --> 00:09:53,210
Changing fourteen to fifteen turned infinitely many solutions into none.

139
00:09:55,233 --> 00:09:56,106
The board's favourite.

140
00:09:56,467 --> 00:09:59,031
The determinant is lambda minus three.

141
00:09:59,233 --> 00:10:06,169
If lambda is not three, the determinant is not zero: a unique solution, whatever mu is.

142
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:14,399
If lambda is three, row reduce: the last row becomes zero, zero, zero, mu minus ten.

143
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:19,992
So if mu is not ten, zero equals something non zero: no solution.

144
00:10:19,992 --> 00:10:24,298
If mu is ten, zero equals zero: infinitely many.

145
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:28,686
Pause and think.

146
00:10:28,686 --> 00:10:31,369
The determinant of A is zero.

147
00:10:31,369 --> 00:10:32,673
What can you say for certain?

148
00:10:39,567 --> 00:10:41,652
It has no unique solution.

149
00:10:41,652 --> 00:10:47,030
It may have none, or infinitely many: only row reduction decides.

150
00:10:47,030 --> 00:10:53,714
Always inconsistent is wrong: a homogeneous system with a zero determinant has infinitely many.

151
00:10:55,700 --> 00:10:56,574
One more.

152
00:10:56,574 --> 00:10:57,861
How is D y built?

153
00:11:04,733 --> 00:11:09,097
The second column, the y column, replaced by the constants.

154
00:11:09,097 --> 00:11:11,072
Replace a column, never a row.

155
00:11:13,267 --> 00:11:14,375
Quick recap.

156
00:11:14,375 --> 00:11:26,159
Write A X equals B. Cramer replaces a column by the constants and divides by D. Gauss row reduces the augmented matrix; Gauss Jordan goes on to the identity.

157
00:11:26,159 --> 00:11:29,922
The inverse method left multiplies by A inverse.

158
00:11:29,922 --> 00:11:35,288
And the last row tells you: one solution, infinitely many, or none.

159
00:11:39,467 --> 00:11:42,086
That was systems of linear equations.

160
00:11:42,086 --> 00:11:47,372
Next, properties of a triangle: the sine, cosine and projection laws.

161
00:11:47,372 --> 00:11:47,942
See you there.

